Fix assumptions of a 32-bit int (docs/M2-REPORT.md, section 5; M1's
patches/0003 for Tiny Tcl, which shares the core code).

- The "end" list index (Tcl_GetListIndex) and "read" with no count used
  1<<14 as "no limit": Tcl 6.7 has 1<<30, which the port lowered to
  16384 for a 16-bit int, so "read $f" returned only the first 16384
  bytes of a larger file without an error. "lrange ... end" used
  1<<30, which is 0. Use INT_MAX for all three.
- Tcl_GetInt converted strtol's long result to int silently, so
  "lindex $l 65536" was "lindex $l 0". Report "integer value too large
  to represent" instead.
- incr added in int, so "incr x 70000" wrapped, and 32767 + 1 was
  -32768. Add in long, through the new TclGetLong, as expr does: incr
  has the 32 bits it has on a 32-bit host.
- format read integer fields with Tcl_GetInt into the low half of
  oneWordValue, a far char pointer, and passed the pointer to sprintf:
  "%D" printed the stale high half, and "%d" could not show 40000. Read
  every integer field as a long with TclGetLong and format it as
  "%l..." (or "%h..." for "h"); "%c" takes an int made from it.
- Tcl_EvalFile (source) allocated (unsigned) st_size + 1 bytes and,
  after the port's change, wrote the terminating zero at the count
  read() returned: a 65535-byte script allocated 0 bytes and overran
  the heap, and a read error (-1) wrote before the block. A file of
  INT_MAX bytes or more is now an error ("file too large"; Tcl 6.7 has
  no line-by-line reader like Tiny Tcl's), and a read error is
  reported.

